    "lawCode": "Amd §§8-400, 9-209, 10-106, 10-114, 11-202 & 11-212, El L; amd §4, Chap 237 of 2005",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5595\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to military voting, and\nto amend chapter 237 of the laws of 2005 amending the election law\nrelating to military voting, in relation to making such provisions\npermanent\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo make permanent a current law affecting military voters that would\notherwise expire December 31, 2009, to make it easier for both\nmilitary and other overseas voters to apply for and obtain a paper\nballot for primary, general and special elections and to expand the\ntime frame for returning such ballots.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 of the bill amends election law § 8-400(2)(d) to provide\nthat military voters and other overseas voters may request a paper\nballot as allowed in subsequent sections of the bill, i.e. by\nfacsimile transmission or electronic mail.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends election law § 9-209(a) to allow boards\nof elections to fix a time for casting and canvassing paper ballots no\nmore than fourteen days after a general or special election and no\nmore than eight days after a primary election.\n\nSection 3 of the bill adds a new subdivision 3 to election law §\n10-106 to provide that a military voter may apply for a military\nballot by facsimile transmission pursuant to the Uniformed and\nOverseas Citizens absentee Voting Act or by electronic mail.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends election law § 10-114(1) to provide that\na military ballot will be counted if a) it is postmarked or dated and\nwitnessed no later than the day before the election and b) it is\nreceived by the board of elections no later than seven days following\na primary election or thirteen days following a general or special\nelection.\n\nSection 5 of the bill adds a new paragraph d to election law §\n11-202(1) to provide that an overseas voter may apply for a special\nfederal ballot by facsimile transmission pursuant to the Uniformed and\nOverseas Citizens absentee Voting Act or by electronic mail.\n\nSection 6 of the bill amends election law § 11-212 to provide that a\nspecial federal ballot cast by an overseas voter will be counted if a)\nit is postmarked or endorsed by a United States government agency no\nlater than the day before the election and b) it is received by the\nboard of elections no later than seven days following a primary\nelection or thirteen days following a general or special election.\n\nSection 7 of the bill amends section 4 of chapter 237 of the laws of\n2005 to repeal the sunset provision and make that chapter's provisions\npermanent.\n\nSection 8 of the bill is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe federal Unifonned and Overseas Citizens Absentee Voting Act was\nenacted in 1986 to promote the opportunity for military and other\noverseas voters to timely apply for and obtain paper ballots that will\nbe cast and counted. In 2001, the United States General Accounting\nOffice estimated that the act covered 6.1 million citizens, including\n2.7 million active military personnel and their relatives. Among\nmilitary personnel who reported not voting in 2004, 30 percent said\n\nthey were not able to vote because their ballots never arrived or\narrived too late.\n\nAccording to the United States Election Assistance Commission, in\n2006, only 26.5 percent of the roughly 1 million absentee ballots\nrequested by military and overseas Americans were cast and counted.\nIn early 2009, the Unites State Senate Rules and Administration\nCommittee, Chaired by Senator Charles Schumer of New York, surveyed\nelection offices in seven states having high numbers of military\npersonnel (California, Florida, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Texas,\nWashington and West Virginia) with the assistance of the Congressional\nResearch Service. The survey found that of the 441,000 absentee\nballots requested by eligible voters living abroad in 2008, one\nquarter had not been counted because they were lost, returned as\n\"undeliverable\" or rejected for various reasons.\n\nThe Pew Center on the States recently issued a report titled No Time\nto Vote: Challenges Facing America's Overseas Military Voters. The\nreport concluded that 16 states, or approximately onethird do not\nprovide enough time for military and overseas voters to complete the\nabsentee ballot process, The report made suggested changes to the\nelection law for each state that would promote the timely receipt of\npaper ballots by military and overseas voters. New York State has\ntried to address this issue since 2005. In order to promote voting by\nmilitary personnel, New York enacted a law that allows military voters\nto sign their ballot envelopes before a witness rather than obtain a\npostmark and extended the time frame for receipt of such ballots by\nthe board of elections to 13 days after a general election. This law\ncontained a sunset provision and has been reenacted each year since\n2005. It is set to expire on December 31, 2009. This bill is an\nattempt to further promote voting by military and other overseas\nvoters. Two of the Pew Center's suggestions for New York are contained\nin chapter 237 of the laws of2005 law and would be made pennanent by\nthis bill. In addition, this bill adopts additional Pew Center\nsuggestions, extends all of the changes to overseas voters in addition\nto military voters and allows additional time for receipt of the\ncompleted ballot after special elections for the first time.\n\nExtending the time to receive military and other overseas ballots will\nbetter ensure that no American living overseas will be disenfranchised\nin New York because of the distance and time it takes to send mail\nfrom the far comers of the world.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nThis makes pennanent chapter 237 of the laws of 2005 and adds new\nprovisions regarding military and other overseas voters.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone to the state. The federal government provides a toll free\nfacsimile number for use by military and overseas federal voters.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
